{"corpus":"twowiki_s3h_graph_haiku","question_id":"122324e8089111ebbd72ac1f6bf848b6","graph_arm":"twowiki/hybrid+graph+rerank+metadata","outcome":"success","answer":"The Galloping Kid was directed by Nat Ross; Parachute Nurse was directed by Charles Barton. The evidence does not provide birth dates for either director, so I cannot determine which director is older. I checked evidence [1] and [2] for the film-director links, and [4]–[8] for possible director birth dates, but none match Nat Ross or Charles Barton.\nAnswer: Insufficient information","snapshot":{"index_generation":"lance-12239","embedding_model":"voyageai/voyage-4-large","vector_weight":1.0,"bm25_weight":1.0,"rrf_k":60,"candidate_limit":32,"final_limit":8,"active_filter":null,"result_hash":"f065b3f68f04912947739f6eec8b5bddb72db0152c972775cc978a229980eab3","retrieved_chunks":[{"chunk_id":"8b3a2282-6014-433e-8cb3-25ff35ed1e8d:0","document_id":"8b3a2282-6014-433e-8cb3-25ff35ed1e8d","title":"The_Galloping_Kid.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"The Galloping Kid\nThe Galloping Kid is a 1922 American western film directed by Nat Ross and featuring Hoot Gibson. It is not known whether the film currently survives.","is_truncated":false,"score":0.6202588677406311,"rank":1,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"d312c804-901f-4100-ab83-cfaf45ff606e:0","document_id":"d312c804-901f-4100-ab83-cfaf45ff606e","title":"Parachute_Nurse.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"Parachute Nurse\nParachute Nurse is a 1942 Columbia Pictures film about the Aerial Nurse Corps. The film was directed by Charles Barton.","is_truncated":false,"score":0.6180669069290161,"rank":2,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"41fcb208-5acc-4152-a349-aea10878201d:0","document_id":"41fcb208-5acc-4152-a349-aea10878201d","title":"The_Galloping_Cowboy.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"The Galloping Cowboy\nThe Galloping Cowboy is a 1926 American silent western film directed by William James Craft and starring Bill Cody and Edmund Cobb.","is_truncated":false,"score":0.505325436592102,"rank":3,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"ba441534-fb0d-4538-a81a-5ddec9247c64:0","document_id":"ba441534-fb0d-4538-a81a-5ddec9247c64","title":"Lesley_Selander.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"Lesley Selander\nLesley Selander( May 26, 1900 – December 5, 1979) was an American film director of Westerns and adventure movies. His career as director, spanning 127 feature films and dozens of TV episodes, lasted from 1936- 68. Before that, Selander was assistant director on films such as\" The Cat and the Fiddle\"( 1934),\" A Night at the Opera\"( 1935), and Fritz Lang's\" Fury\"( 1936). To this day Selander remains one of the most prolific directors of feature Westerns in cinema history, having taken the helm","is_truncated":true,"score":0.43379831314086914,"rank":4,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"d94de9b2-dcaf-49b1-a5f7-79c2afc00d2c:0","document_id":"d94de9b2-dcaf-49b1-a5f7-79c2afc00d2c","title":"Stephanie_Rothman.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"Stephanie Rothman\nStephanie Rothman( born November 9, 1936 in Paterson, New Jersey) is an American film director, producer, and screenwriter, known for her low- budget independent exploitation films made in the 1960s and 1970s, especially\" The Student Nurses\"( 1970) and\" Terminal Island\"( 1974).","is_truncated":false,"score":0.4336533546447754,"rank":5,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"0a5e0120-5a56-4c67-8d20-89026376dfd8:0","document_id":"0a5e0120-5a56-4c67-8d20-89026376dfd8","title":"Frank_McDonald__director_.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"Frank McDonald (director)\nFrank McDonald( November 9, 1899 Baltimore, Maryland – March 8, 1980 Oxnard, California) was an American film and television director, active from 1935 to 1966. He directed more than 100 films, including many Westerns starring Gene Autry and Roy Rogers, and numerous TV show episodes. He is interred at Conejo Mountain Memorial Park in Camarillo, California.","is_truncated":false,"score":0.4290245771408081,"rank":6,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"c591c232-0ae8-4a76-a1f2-4d195026eb12:0","document_id":"c591c232-0ae8-4a76-a1f2-4d195026eb12","title":"Edward_Sedgwick.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"Edward Sedgwick\nEdward Sedgwick( November 7, 1889 – March 7, 1953) was an American film director, writer, actor and producer.","is_truncated":false,"score":0.4255819022655487,"rank":7,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"4d344f30-62ef-4cd1-bddf-36bbaf3a9371:0","document_id":"4d344f30-62ef-4cd1-bddf-36bbaf3a9371","title":"Norman_Taurog.txt","section_path":"Root","excerpt":"Norman Taurog\nNorman Rae Taurog( February 23, 1899 – April 7, 1981) was an American film director and screenwriter. From 1920 to 1968, Taurog directed 180 films. At the age of 32, he received the Academy Award for Best Director for\" Skippy\"( 1931). He is the second youngest person ever to win the award after Damien Chazelle, who won for\" La La Land\" in 2017. He was later nominated for Best Director for the film\" Boys Town\"( 1938). He directed some of the best- known actors of the twentieth century, includin","is_truncated":true,"score":0.4255536198616028,"rank":8,"content_type":"text/plain","graph_boosted":false}],"retrieval_mode":"hybrid","pre_truncation_ranking":[{"chunk_id":"8b3a2282-6014-433e-8cb3-25ff35ed1e8d:0","document_id":"8b3a2282-6014-433e-8cb3-25ff35ed1e8d","fused_rank":1,"vector_rank":2,"bm25_rank":2,"graph_rank":null,"graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"d312c804-901f-4100-ab83-cfaf45ff606e:0","document_id":"d312c804-901f-4100-ab83-cfaf45ff606e","fused_rank":2,"vector_rank":1,"bm25_rank":1,"graph_rank":null,"graph_boosted":false},{"chunk_id":"41fcb208-5acc-4152-a349-aea10878201d:0","document_id":"41fcb208-5acc-4152-a349-aea10878201d","fused_rank":3,"vector_rank":6,"bm25_rank":3,"graph_